#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ptest — one test command for every project and every agent.

Agents call `ptest` (scoped) or `ptest --full` (whole suite) and never learn where
it ran. Scoped runs stay local with capped workers so six concurrent sessions do not
oversubscribe the box — unless the path is large enough to earn its own container
(see remote_scoped_min_files below); full runs go to the configured backend.

  ptest PATH [ARGS...]      scoped run, workers capped. PATH must really scope:
                            bare `ptest`, `ptest .`, and any directory the whole
                            suite lives under (`tests`, `src`) are `--full` in
                            disguise and are REFUSED, for every runner alike.
                            A `-k`/`-m`/`--lf` filter counts as scoping.
                            A path covering >= remote_scoped_min_files test
                            files goes to the backend instead of running here,
                            which trades verbatim output for a summarized
                            digest (--local keeps this run, and its full
                            output, here).
  ptest --full              full suite, remote if configured, else local
  ptest --full --fresh      force a new remote execution for benchmarking
  ptest --local --full      force local even when a remote is configured
  ptest where               show which project/backend resolved for this directory
  ptest doctor              check config, caps, backend reachability, quota
  ptest status              show live Spot queued jobs, working jobs, and servers on
  ptest result KEY          show one durable Spot result; add --output to page its log
  ptest register            print a config stanza for the current repo

Config: ~/.config/ptest/config.toml   (see `ptest register`)

Design rules that must not be broken — agents depend on them:
  * the exit code is the test run's exit code, always
  * test output goes to stdout verbatim, never wrapped or summarised
  * it blocks until the run finishes
  * an unreachable backend falls back to local with one warning, never hangs
"""

